// Tauride Garden data: the DESIGN VALUES of the furniture, the fence and gates, the lanterns, the monuments, the fountain, the orangery, the pitch / playground / fitness fittings (docs/TAURIDE-REMAKE.md, phase T6).
// Hand-authored (NOT generated by tools/tauride/build.mjs), pure data: no imports. Every number is a DESIGN value (no survey): data/furniture.js holds what OpenStreetMap maps (positions, ids, the fence ways and their
// openings); this file says how big and how far apart, which colours and which counts. tools/tauride/furniture-test.mjs asserts the rules written here on the real built module, each with a mutant.
// Frame: metres from ORIGIN, +X east, +Z south (-Z is north), the frame of the whole project.

/** BENCHES (data/furniture.js BENCHES, 132 OSM nodes; one has no backrest = a granite bench): the strip's park bench of props/furniture.js benchPark (black cast ends, orange-brown slats) in a lean 52-triangle model.
 *  ORIENTATION RULE: a bench faces the nearest way ribbon (a non-bridge, non-steps way of data/paths.js) within `faceWay` m of its centre (the front looks at the ribbon's nearest point); else the nearest water within
 *  `faceWater` m (the front looks at the shore); else the garden centre `centre`. NUDGE: the benches are OSM nodes beside the alleys; one whose footprint (len x depth, turned to face the way) would stand on a ribbon
 *  or within `edge` m of its edge, or on a footprint / the water, moves along the normal of the nearest way away from it in `nudgeStep` m steps up to `nudgeMax` m (`node` records the move); one that cannot be
 *  placed within `nudgeMax` m stays where it is (and the test reports it). */
export const BENCH = {
  len: 1.8, depth: 0.55, h: 0.9, seatH: 0.46,
  faceWay: 6, faceWater: 30, centre: [1210, -1520],
  edge: 0.3, nudgeStep: 0.15, nudgeMax: 3.0,
  iron: '#1c1e1d', wood: '#b8793a', woodD: '#7a4a2a',
  mass: 40, hitK: 0.25, far: 220,
};
/** BINS (data/furniture.js BINS, 167 nodes): the strip's black cast-iron urn (props/furniture.js binCast: a vase 0.78 m high on a round foot), hexagonal. The same nudge rule as the benches (a circle of `r`). */
export const BIN = { r: 0.3, h: 0.78, edge: 0.3, nudgeStep: 0.15, nudgeMax: 4.5, iron: '#1c1e1d', mass: 14, hitK: 0.12, far: 170 };

/** LANTERNS: the garden lights itself (a claimed way loses its street lamps; the mapped data has no lamp in the garden: LAMPS_IN_GARDEN 0). The 17 `lit` ways of data/paths.js (bridge ways and steps excepted) get a cast-iron
 *  garden lantern (a green-black pole 4.2 m with a collar, a four-sided glass lantern and a pyramid roof: the strip's landmarksE lantern, scaled) every `spacing` m along the way, on ALTERNATE sides, `offset` m beyond the
 *  ribbon's edge (the drawn ribbon is the nominal half width +- `widthVar` plus the ragged edge `rag`, paths-draped.js), starting `start` m from the way's first vertex. A candidate is dropped when it stands on a
 *  ribbon of ANY way, on a stair / bridge head / deck (axis + `deck`), within `clear` m of a bench / bin / gate / pier / another lantern, in the water or its bank band, in a footprint, outside the garden, within `fence`
 *  m of the fence line or within `pose` m of a camera pose (data/vegetation.js CLEARINGS: a pole in the middle of a QA frame). Night: the lantern's glow follows the page's uNight (the strip's lamps); a light pool (additive, radius `pool`) lies on the ground. */
export const LANTERN = {
  spacing: 27, start: 8, offset: 0.6, widthVar: 0.08, rag: 0.17, h: 4.2,
  clear: { bench: 1.3, bin: 0.9, gate: 4, pier: 0.8, lantern: 12, deck: 2.2, fence: 1.2, footprint: 1.0, stair: 2.0, pose: 6 },
  poolR: 4.6, poolK: 0.5,
  iron: '#25382d', ironL: '#33493b', glass: '#fff0cc', mass: 160, anchored: 18, hitK: 0.5, far: 260, farGlow: 420,
};

/** THE FENCE (the cast-iron fence of 190 cm that replaced the log fence, architect Volkov, 1860s .. 1896: docs/research/tauride-reference.md; data/furniture.js FENCES, 14 mapped ways, 897 panels of <= 2.7 m):
 *  the panel is a granite plinth (0.3 m wide, 0.22 m above the ground and `skirt` m below it so that the foot reaches the floor where the garden's floor lies below the street), two rails and the pickets with spear tips
 *  (an alpha-cut plane, the strip's picket pattern of landmarksE / landmarksF). Piers (granite, 0.5 x 0.5 x 2.2 m with a capstone) every `pier.min` .. `pier.max` panels, at every corner (turn above `turn` degrees),
 *  at the ends of a fence and on both sides of every opening. THE BASE: the fence stands on the STREET side: y = max(0, gardenHeight) (the relief is zero within 8 m of the outline; along the quay stretches of the
 *  lake's north shore the floor lies at -0.45, the quay top at 0): `quayShift` m outward where the shore distance is under `shoreNear`. A panel in the water (the stub w112853216 reaches 1.7 m into it) is not built. */
export const FENCE = {
  panelM: 2.7, h: 1.9, plinthW: 0.3, plinthH: 0.22, skirt: 0.3, railW: 0.06, railH: 0.055, rails: [0.55, 1.72],
  collider: { thick: 0.16, y0: -0.5, y1: 1.9 },
  pier: { w: 0.5, h: 2.2, cap: 0.62, capH: 0.16, min: 3, max: 5, turn: 15, collider: 0.5 },
  quayShift: 0.18, shoreNear: 0.3,
  openTol: 0.12,
  iron: '#1f2a24', ironL: '#2d3d33', granite: '#8f8a82', graniteL: '#aaa59b', stone: '#b6ab94',
  far: 650,
};
